{% doc %}
  Mounts the toast element.

  One per page, in the overlay group, so `toast()` from any module has somewhere
  to render. It carries no content: everything it shows arrives through
  `toast.show()`, which is why there is no markup here beyond the element itself.

  The custom properties below are the whole theming surface. The toast lives in a
  shadow root — the only component in this theme that does — because it appears
  above everything while a customer is mid-task, and a merchant's Custom CSS
  accidentally matching `.toast`, or an app's `z-index`, should not be able to
  break a confirmation. Everywhere else in this theme, merchant CSS reaching a
  component is a feature; here it is a hazard.

  `part` attributes expose the pieces that are genuinely meant to be styled:
  `container`, `icon`, `body`, `message`, `action`, `close`.

  @example
    {% render 'toast-notification' %}
{% enddoc %}

<toast-notification
  id="Toast"
  style="
    --toast-background: {{ settings.colors_toast_background | default: '#1a1a1a' }};
    --toast-text: {{ settings.colors_toast_text | default: '#ffffff' }};
    --toast-error-background: {{ settings.colors_toast_error | default: '#8a1b1b' }};
    --toast-radius: {{ settings.buttons_radius | default: 8 }}px;
    --toast-offset: {{ settings.toast_offset | default: 20 }}px;
  "
></toast-notification>
